1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,581
This is ECE 320, video 16. The topic is pseudo-LRU replacement, or PLRU. It is unit 8 of the slides.

2
00:00:11,031 --> 00:00:19,031
After this video, you can do four things. One: explain why true LRU is expensive, by counting the bits.

3
00:00:19,481 --> 00:00:25,348
Two: read the PLRU tree bits, B0, B1 and B2, of a 4-way set.

4
00:00:25,798 --> 00:00:28,721
Three: walk the tree to find the victim on a miss.

5
00:00:29,171 --> 00:00:34,973
Four: update the bits on a hit or a fill, and compare PLRU with true LRU.

6
00:00:35,423 --> 00:00:50,037
First, recall true LRU from lecture 7. In a 4-way set, each block has an age count. Here the ages are 1, 3, 0 and 2. This is a row of the example on slide 45.

7
00:00:50,487 --> 00:00:59,191
The age goes from 0 to 3. These 4 values need 2 bits: 0 0, 0 1, 1 0 and 1 1.

8
00:00:59,641 --> 00:01:04,035
Now a hit in block 3. Its age was 2. It becomes 0.

9
00:01:04,485 --> 00:01:12,059
The ages lower than 2 get plus 1. Block 0 goes from 1 to 2. Block 2 goes from 0 to 1.

10
00:01:12,509 --> 00:01:18,077
Block 1 has age 3. That is not lower than 2, so block 1 keeps age 3.

11
00:01:18,527 --> 00:01:21,300
So one access changed three age counts.

12
00:01:21,750 --> 00:01:31,414
On a miss, the cache replaces the block with the largest age. Here that is block 1, with age 3. It is the least recently used block.

13
00:01:31,864 --> 00:01:38,840
So true LRU needs 2 bits for each of the 4 blocks. 4 times 2 is 8 bits per set.

14
00:01:39,290 --> 00:01:43,237
And every access must compare the age of every block in the set.

15
00:01:43,687 --> 00:01:57,319
Slide 2 of unit 8 says that true LRU is expensive to implement. Count the bits. A 4-way set has 4 age counts, and each has 2 bits. 4 times 2 is 8 bits per set.

16
00:01:57,769 --> 00:02:09,246
With N ways, each age goes from 0 to N minus 1. That needs log 2 of N bits. So true LRU needs N times log 2 of N bits per set.

17
00:02:09,696 --> 00:02:15,115
For 8 ways, each age has 3 bits. 8 times 3 is 24 bits.

18
00:02:15,565 --> 00:02:21,943
For 16 ways, each age has 4 bits. 16 times 4 is 64 bits.

19
00:02:22,393 --> 00:02:28,025
PLRU needs only N minus 1 bits per set. Slide 3 gives this rule.

20
00:02:28,475 --> 00:02:34,854
That is 3 bits for 4 ways, 7 bits for 8 ways, and 15 bits for 16 ways.

21
00:02:35,304 --> 00:02:45,928
For 2 ways, true LRU needs 2 times 1, which is 2 bits. PLRU needs 1 bit. With 2 ways, this 1 bit gives exact LRU.

22
00:02:46,378 --> 00:03:00,693
The work per access is also smaller. Here is an 8-way PLRU tree with some example bit values. It has one bit in each node: 8 minus 1, which is 7 bits, B0 to B6.

23
00:03:01,143 --> 00:03:04,919
The nodes are in 3 levels, from the root down to the ways.

24
00:03:05,369 --> 00:03:11,427
On a miss, the cache reads only the bits on one path. B0 is 1, so go right.

25
00:03:11,877 --> 00:03:13,861
B2 is 1, so go right.

26
00:03:14,311 --> 00:03:21,458
B6 is 0, so go left. The victim is way 6. The cache read only 3 of the 7 bits.

27
00:03:21,908 --> 00:03:32,319
After the fill of way 6, the cache walks up and writes the same 3 bits. Each bit points away from way 6. B6 becomes 1, to point right.

28
00:03:32,769 --> 00:03:35,350
B2 becomes 0, to point left.

29
00:03:35,800 --> 00:03:38,531
B0 becomes 0, to point left.

30
00:03:38,981 --> 00:03:41,093
The other 4 bits do not change.

31
00:03:41,543 --> 00:03:51,505
True LRU with 8 ways has 8 ages of 3 bits. It must compare all 8 ages on every access, and all of them can change.

32
00:03:51,955 --> 00:04:01,513
This is why the slides call PLRU a hardware friendly approximation of LRU. It uses fewer bits, and its updates are simple.

33
00:04:01,963 --> 00:04:09,515
PLRU is a hardware friendly approximation of LRU. It uses fewer bits than true LRU.

34
00:04:09,965 --> 00:04:15,554
PLRU keeps the least recently used information of one set in a binary tree.

35
00:04:16,004 --> 00:04:18,201
Each node of the tree is one bit.

36
00:04:18,651 --> 00:04:20,934
The leaves of the tree are the ways.

37
00:04:21,384 --> 00:04:31,923
On a miss, the cache must find a victim. Only then does it walk the tree. It starts at the root and follows the bits down to one way. That way is the victim.

38
00:04:32,373 --> 00:04:40,159
On a hit, the cache does not walk down. It only updates the PLRU bits on the path from the hit way up to the root.

39
00:04:40,609 --> 00:04:54,156
While it walks up, it sets each bit to point away from the way that was just used. The dashed arrows show this. So the way just used cannot be the next victim. This keeps the LRU information.

40
00:04:54,606 --> 00:05:02,030
A fill after a miss updates the bits in the same way. The two bits above the new block now point away from it.

41
00:05:02,480 --> 00:05:11,739
Slide 3 uses a 4-way set-associative cache. The leaves of the tree are the 4 ways, W0 to W3.

42
00:05:12,189 --> 00:05:25,714
The tree needs N minus 1 bits, where N is the number of ways. There is one bit in each node. So 4 ways need 4 minus 1, which is 3 bits: B0, B1 and B2.

43
00:05:26,164 --> 00:05:31,988
The slides write the state of the set as B0, B1, B2 in square brackets.

44
00:05:32,438 --> 00:05:36,833
B0 is the root. It chooses between the two halves of the set.

45
00:05:37,283 --> 00:05:40,675
The left side of B0 is ways 0 and 1.

46
00:05:41,125 --> 00:05:44,261
The right side of B0 is ways 2 and 3.

47
00:05:44,711 --> 00:05:47,932
B1 chooses between way 0 and way 1.

48
00:05:48,382 --> 00:05:51,646
B2 chooses between way 2 and way 3.

49
00:05:52,096 --> 00:05:59,136
The value of a bit tells which side is LRU. 0 means that the left side of the tree is LRU.

50
00:05:59,586 --> 00:06:02,957
1 means that the right side of the tree is LRU.

51
00:06:03,407 --> 00:06:10,020
An example: the state 0 1 1. The dashed arrows show the side that each bit points to.

52
00:06:10,470 --> 00:06:15,547
B0 is 0, so the left half, ways 0 and 1, is LRU.

53
00:06:15,997 --> 00:06:20,435
B1 is 1, so way 1 is the LRU way of the left pair.

54
00:06:20,885 --> 00:06:25,535
B2 is 1, so way 3 is the LRU way of the right pair.

55
00:06:25,985 --> 00:06:29,228
Slide 4 starts with the state 0 0 0.

56
00:06:29,678 --> 00:06:45,465
To show the cache set, we give the blocks names. The set holds A, B, C and D in ways 0 to 3. These names are ours. The slides show only the ways. Now block E misses. The cache must replace a block.

57
00:06:45,915 --> 00:06:49,840
Walk down from the root. B0 is 0, so go left.

58
00:06:50,290 --> 00:06:54,429
B1 is 0, so go left. The walk ends at way 0.

59
00:06:54,879 --> 00:07:00,148
Way 0 is chosen to be replaced. Block A, in way 0, is evicted.

60
00:07:00,598 --> 00:07:05,761
Block E fills way 0. Now walk up from way 0, and update the bits.

61
00:07:06,211 --> 00:07:11,651
Way 0 is on the left of B1. So B1 becomes 1, to point right.

62
00:07:12,101 --> 00:07:19,418
Go up to B0. Way 0 is on the left of B0. So B0 becomes 1, to point right.

63
00:07:19,868 --> 00:07:28,273
B2 is not on the path, so it keeps its value, 0. The new state is 1 1 0. This is the state on the slide.

64
00:07:28,723 --> 00:07:37,257
Now both bits on the path point away from way 0. So the next miss cannot choose way 0, which holds the newest block.

65
00:07:37,707 --> 00:07:41,205
Slide 5 continues from the state 1 1 0.

66
00:07:41,655 --> 00:07:52,109
The set now holds E, B, C and D. Block F is not in the set, so it misses. Find the victim and the new state. Pause the video and try it.

67
00:07:52,559 --> 00:07:56,292
Walk down from the root. B0 is 1, so go right.

68
00:07:56,742 --> 00:08:00,753
B2 is 0, so go left. The walk ends at way 2.

69
00:08:01,203 --> 00:08:06,152
Way 2 is chosen to be replaced. Block C, in way 2, is evicted.

70
00:08:06,602 --> 00:08:11,359
Block F fills way 2. Now walk up from way 2, and update the bits.

71
00:08:11,809 --> 00:08:17,079
Way 2 is on the left of B2. So B2 becomes 1, to point right.

72
00:08:17,529 --> 00:08:24,803
Go up to B0. Way 2 is on the right of B0. So B0 becomes 0, to point left.

73
00:08:25,253 --> 00:08:31,184
B1 is not on the path, so it keeps its value, 1. The new state is 0 1 1.

74
00:08:31,634 --> 00:08:34,706
Slide 6 starts with the state 0 1 1.

75
00:08:35,156 --> 00:08:41,257
The set holds E, B, F and D. Block G misses. Pause the video and try it.

76
00:08:41,707 --> 00:08:45,633
Walk down from the root. B0 is 0, so go left.

77
00:08:46,083 --> 00:08:49,709
B1 is 1, so go right. The walk ends at way 1.

78
00:08:50,159 --> 00:08:54,959
Way 1 is chosen to be replaced. Block B, in way 1, is evicted.

79
00:08:55,409 --> 00:09:00,209
Block G fills way 1. Now walk up from way 1, and update the bits.

80
00:09:00,659 --> 00:09:05,929
Way 1 is on the right of B1. So B1 becomes 0, to point left.

81
00:09:06,379 --> 00:09:13,376
Go up to B0. Way 1 is on the left of B0. So B0 becomes 1, to point right.

82
00:09:13,826 --> 00:09:20,119
B2 is not on the path, so it keeps its value, 1. The new state is 1 0 1.

83
00:09:20,569 --> 00:09:23,641
Slide 7 starts with the state 1 0 1.

84
00:09:24,091 --> 00:09:30,043
The set holds E, G, F and D. Block H misses. Pause the video and try it.

85
00:09:30,493 --> 00:09:34,227
Walk down from the root. B0 is 1, so go right.

86
00:09:34,677 --> 00:09:38,538
B2 is 1, so go right. The walk ends at way 3.

87
00:09:38,988 --> 00:09:44,193
Way 3 is chosen to be replaced. Block D, in way 3, is evicted.

88
00:09:44,643 --> 00:09:49,571
Block H fills way 3. Now walk up from way 3, and update the bits.

89
00:09:50,021 --> 00:09:55,461
Way 3 is on the right of B2. So B2 becomes 0, to point left.

90
00:09:55,911 --> 00:10:03,186
Go up to B0. Way 3 is on the right of B0. So B0 becomes 0, to point left.

91
00:10:03,636 --> 00:10:10,143
B1 is not on the path, so it keeps its value, 0. The new state is 0 0 0.

92
00:10:10,593 --> 00:10:22,561
Be careful with the text of slide 7. It writes B1 equals 0, but the bit on the path is B2. The drawing and the result, 0 0 0, are correct.

93
00:10:23,011 --> 00:10:26,744
Slide 8 starts again with the state 0 0 0.

94
00:10:27,194 --> 00:10:33,125
The set holds E, G, F and H. Block I misses. Pause the video and try it.

95
00:10:33,575 --> 00:10:37,500
Walk down from the root. B0 is 0, so go left.

96
00:10:37,950 --> 00:10:42,089
B1 is 0, so go left. The walk ends at way 0.

97
00:10:42,539 --> 00:10:47,851
Way 0 is chosen to be replaced. Block E, in way 0, is evicted.

98
00:10:48,301 --> 00:10:53,357
Block I fills way 0. Now walk up from way 0, and update the bits.

99
00:10:53,807 --> 00:10:59,247
Way 0 is on the left of B1. So B1 becomes 1, to point right.

100
00:10:59,697 --> 00:11:07,014
Go up to B0. Way 0 is on the left of B0. So B0 becomes 1, to point right.

101
00:11:07,464 --> 00:11:17,000
B2 is not on the path, so it keeps its value, 0. The new state is 1 1 0. This is the same result as on slide 4.

102
00:11:17,450 --> 00:11:25,173
The text of slide 8 has two typos. First, it writes B2 where it means B1, the second bit of the walk.

103
00:11:25,623 --> 00:11:38,892
Second, it writes B0 equals 0, B2 equals 1. The drawing shows B0 equals 1 and B1 equals 1. The result on the slide, 1 1 0, is correct.

104
00:11:39,342 --> 00:11:46,211
Put the misses of slides 4 to 8 in a row. The end state of each slide is the start state of the next one.

105
00:11:46,661 --> 00:11:52,101
The victims are way 0, way 2, way 1, way 3, and then way 0 again.

106
00:11:52,551 --> 00:11:55,495
So the first 4 misses replace each way once.

107
00:11:55,945 --> 00:12:05,609
Rule 1, for a miss. Start at the root. If the bit is 0, go left. If it is 1, go right. The way at the end of the walk is the victim.

108
00:12:06,059 --> 00:12:13,995
Rule 2, for a hit or a fill. Set each bit on the path from the used way to the root to point away from that way.

109
00:12:14,445 --> 00:12:18,200
The table lists the result of rule 2 for each used way.

110
00:12:18,650 --> 00:12:26,735
For example, a use of way 2. Way 2 is on the left of B2, so B2 becomes 1, to point at way 3.

111
00:12:27,185 --> 00:12:33,607
Way 2 is on the right of B0, so B0 becomes 0, to point at the left half.

112
00:12:34,057 --> 00:12:41,523
A dash in the table means that the bit is not on the path. It keeps its value. Here B1 keeps its value.

113
00:12:41,973 --> 00:12:50,016
So each row has 2 values: each access writes exactly 2 bits. Their values depend only on the way number.

114
00:12:50,466 --> 00:12:57,485
Slide 9 shows hits. It starts with the state 0 1 1. This is the state after slide 5.

115
00:12:57,935 --> 00:13:06,020
The set holds E, B, F and D. Block F hits in way 2. Find the new state. Pause the video and try it.

116
00:13:06,470 --> 00:13:10,822
On a hit, the cache does not walk down. It walks up from way 2.

117
00:13:11,272 --> 00:13:17,821
Way 2 is on the left of B2. B2 must be 1, to point right. It is already 1.

118
00:13:18,271 --> 00:13:27,189
Go up to B0. Way 2 is on the right of B0. B0 must be 0, to point left. It is already 0.

119
00:13:27,639 --> 00:13:35,852
B1 is not on the path, so it keeps its value, 1. The new state is 0 1 1. The slide writes, unchanged.

120
00:13:36,302 --> 00:13:40,462
Next, block E hits in way 0. Pause the video and try it.

121
00:13:40,912 --> 00:13:45,413
On a hit, the cache does not walk down. It walks up from way 0.

122
00:13:45,863 --> 00:13:52,541
Way 0 is on the left of B1. B1 must be 1, to point right. It is already 1.

123
00:13:52,991 --> 00:14:00,308
Go up to B0. Way 0 is on the left of B0. So B0 becomes 1, to point right.

124
00:14:00,758 --> 00:14:08,673
B2 is not on the path, so it keeps its value, 1. The new state is 1 1 1. This is the state on the slide.

125
00:14:09,123 --> 00:14:13,709
From 1 1 1, look at the next miss. B0 is 1, so go right.

126
00:14:14,159 --> 00:14:18,298
B2 is 1, so go right. The next victim is way 3.

127
00:14:18,748 --> 00:14:23,505
Ways 0 and 2 were just used, and the tree keeps both of them safe.

128
00:14:23,955 --> 00:14:32,830
Slide 11 is a note about the approximation. It starts with the state 1 0 1. This is the state after slide 6.

129
00:14:33,280 --> 00:14:39,915
The set holds E, G, F and D. Block F hits in way 2. Pause the video and try it.

130
00:14:40,365 --> 00:14:44,717
On a hit, the cache does not walk down. It walks up from way 2.

131
00:14:45,167 --> 00:14:51,716
Way 2 is on the left of B2. B2 must be 1, to point right. It is already 1.

132
00:14:52,166 --> 00:14:59,441
Go up to B0. Way 2 is on the right of B0. So B0 becomes 0, to point left.

133
00:14:59,891 --> 00:15:08,573
B1 is not on the path, so it keeps its value, 0. The new state is 0 0 1. This is the state on the slide.

134
00:15:09,023 --> 00:15:17,023
Then the slide asks for a new replacement. A new block, X, misses. Find the victim. Pause the video and try it.

135
00:15:17,473 --> 00:15:21,399
Walk down from the root. B0 is 0, so go left.

136
00:15:21,849 --> 00:15:25,987
B1 is 0, so go left. The walk ends at way 0.

137
00:15:26,437 --> 00:15:31,749
Way 0 is chosen to be replaced. Block E, in way 0, is evicted.

138
00:15:32,199 --> 00:15:37,213
Block X fills way 0. Now walk up from way 0, and update the bits.

139
00:15:37,663 --> 00:15:43,103
Way 0 is on the left of B1. So B1 becomes 1, to point right.

140
00:15:43,553 --> 00:15:50,870
Go up to B0. Way 0 is on the left of B0. So B0 becomes 1, to point right.

141
00:15:51,320 --> 00:15:57,229
B2 is not on the path, so it keeps its value, 1. The new state is 1 1 1.

142
00:15:57,679 --> 00:16:09,754
The slide draws the tree 0 0 1 for this replacement, but it does not show the result. Next, we check if way 0 really holds the least recently used block.

143
00:16:10,204 --> 00:16:15,623
Here is the full history of this set. Each use has a time, t 1 to t 8.

144
00:16:16,073 --> 00:16:20,467
First, A, B, C and D fill the set, at t 1 to t 4.

145
00:16:20,917 --> 00:16:25,077
Then E, F and G miss, on slides 4, 5 and 6.

146
00:16:25,527 --> 00:16:28,386
Then F hits, on slide 11, at t 8.

147
00:16:28,836 --> 00:16:37,156
True LRU needs the last use of each block in the set. Block E, in way 0, was last used at t 5.

148
00:16:37,606 --> 00:16:40,209
Block G was last used at t 7.

149
00:16:40,659 --> 00:16:46,355
Block F was used at t 6 and again at t 8. So its last use is t 8.

150
00:16:46,805 --> 00:16:49,237
Block D was last used at t 4.

151
00:16:49,687 --> 00:16:55,895
True LRU replaces the block with the oldest last use. That is D, in way 3.

152
00:16:56,345 --> 00:17:02,531
Now PLRU, with the state 0 0 1. B0 is 0, so go left.

153
00:17:02,981 --> 00:17:09,467
B1 is 0, so go left again. PLRU replaces way 0. That is block E.

154
00:17:09,917 --> 00:17:18,749
But E was last used at t 5, after D at t 4. So here PLRU does not choose the least recently used block.

155
00:17:19,199 --> 00:17:29,972
The reason: each bit only records which side of its node was used more recently. The hit on F, in way 2, set B0 to 0, to point left.

156
00:17:30,422 --> 00:17:36,950
But the oldest block, D, is on the right side too, next to F. B0 cannot show this.

157
00:17:37,400 --> 00:17:49,709
This part is not on the slides. It shows why 3 bits are only an approximation. Each cell here is one use order of the 4 ways, from the oldest use to the newest use.

158
00:17:50,159 --> 00:18:03,535
The oldest way can be any of the 4 ways. The next one can be any of the other 3, then 2, then 1. So there are 4 times 3 times 2 times 1, which is 24 use orders.

159
00:18:03,985 --> 00:18:17,297
Use the 4 ways in one order, from any start state. The PLRU bits then depend only on the order. 3 bits have 2 times 2 times 2, which is 8 states. Each column is one state.

160
00:18:17,747 --> 00:18:27,625
Each state has 3 orders. For example, the state 0 0 1 has 3 orders. 8 states times 3 orders is 24 orders.

161
00:18:28,075 --> 00:18:42,517
The slide 11 example used the ways in the order W3, W0, W1, W2. This order gives the state 0 0 1, and PLRU replaces way 0.

162
00:18:42,967 --> 00:18:49,239
In the two other orders of this column, way 0 is the oldest. There, PLRU is right.

163
00:18:49,689 --> 00:18:55,321
In the slide 11 order, way 3 is the oldest. There, PLRU is wrong.

164
00:18:55,771 --> 00:19:03,174
The 3 bits cannot tell these 3 orders apart. So PLRU cannot always find the true LRU way.

165
00:19:03,624 --> 00:19:11,453
Now compare the two policies on the misses of slides 4 to 8. Both sets start with A, B, C and D.

166
00:19:11,903 --> 00:19:20,394
The bottom line shows the true LRU use order, from old to new: A, B, C, D. The oldest block is in red.

167
00:19:20,844 --> 00:19:27,585
For this order, the PLRU state is 0 0 0. That is the start state of slide 4.

168
00:19:28,035 --> 00:19:37,187
E misses. Both policies replace A, in way 0. For true LRU, A was the oldest block. E is now the newest block.

169
00:19:37,637 --> 00:19:44,123
F misses. PLRU walks the tree 1 1 0 to way 2. It replaces block C.

170
00:19:44,573 --> 00:19:50,226
True LRU replaces its oldest block, B, in way 1. The victims differ.

171
00:19:50,676 --> 00:19:57,268
G misses. PLRU walks the tree 0 1 1 to way 1. It replaces block B.

172
00:19:57,718 --> 00:20:01,878
True LRU replaces its oldest block, C, in way 2.

173
00:20:02,328 --> 00:20:09,987
H misses. Both policies replace block D, in way 3. D is the oldest block for true LRU.

174
00:20:10,437 --> 00:20:14,661
I misses. Both policies replace block E, in way 0.

175
00:20:15,111 --> 00:20:22,364
Here all accesses miss, so both policies have 5 misses. The victims differ only at F and G.

176
00:20:22,814 --> 00:20:31,902
But between the accesses, the sets hold different blocks. After F, PLRU still holds B, and true LRU still holds C.

177
00:20:32,352 --> 00:20:38,453
A later access to one of these blocks gives a hit in one policy and a miss in the other.

178
00:20:38,903 --> 00:20:57,314
A practice question in exam style. It is not from the slides, but it uses only the slide rules. The state is 1 1 1. Then the set gets a hit on way 3, a miss, a hit on way 1, and a miss. Find each victim and each new state. Pause the video and try it.

179
00:20:57,764 --> 00:21:01,177
First, a hit on way 3. Walk up from way 3.

180
00:21:01,627 --> 00:21:07,067
Way 3 is on the right of B2. So B2 becomes 0, to point left.

181
00:21:07,517 --> 00:21:14,792
Go up to B0. Way 3 is on the right of B0. So B0 becomes 0, to point left.

182
00:21:15,242 --> 00:21:20,255
B1 is not on the path, so it keeps 1. The state is 0 1 0.

183
00:21:20,705 --> 00:21:25,655
Next, a miss. Walk down from the root. B0 is 0, so go left.

184
00:21:26,105 --> 00:21:29,774
B1 is 1, so go right. The victim is way 1.

185
00:21:30,224 --> 00:21:37,755
The new block fills way 1. Way 1 is on the right of B1. So B1 becomes 0, to point left.

186
00:21:38,205 --> 00:21:45,202
Go up to B0. Way 1 is on the left of B0. So B0 becomes 1, to point right.

187
00:21:45,652 --> 00:21:51,007
B2 is not on the path, so it keeps 0. The state is 1 0 0.

188
00:21:51,457 --> 00:21:54,550
Next, a hit on way 1. Walk up from way 1.

189
00:21:55,000 --> 00:22:01,805
Way 1 is on the right of B1. B1 must be 0, to point left. It is already 0.

190
00:22:02,255 --> 00:22:10,511
Go up to B0. Way 1 is on the left of B0. B0 must be 1, to point right. It is already 1.

191
00:22:10,961 --> 00:22:16,572
B2 is not on the path, so it keeps 0. The state stays 1 0 0.

192
00:22:17,022 --> 00:22:21,758
Next, a miss. Walk down from the root. B0 is 1, so go right.

193
00:22:22,208 --> 00:22:26,261
B2 is 0, so go left. The victim is way 2.

194
00:22:26,711 --> 00:22:33,922
The new block fills way 2. Way 2 is on the left of B2. So B2 becomes 1, to point right.

195
00:22:34,372 --> 00:22:41,647
Go up to B0. Way 2 is on the right of B0. So B0 becomes 0, to point left.

196
00:22:42,097 --> 00:22:47,451
B1 is not on the path, so it keeps 0. The state is 0 0 1.

197
00:22:47,901 --> 00:22:54,237
The answer: the victims are way 1 and then way 2. The final state is 0 0 1.

198
00:22:54,687 --> 00:23:02,730
The method: on a miss, walk down. On every access, set the 2 bits on the path to point away from the used way.

199
00:23:03,180 --> 00:23:25,772
A second practice question, also not from the slides. The set holds A, B, C and D, used in that order. So the PLRU state is 0 0 0. The true LRU order is A, B, C, D, from oldest to newest. The accesses are A, E, B and F. Do both policies. Pause the video and try it.

200
00:23:26,222 --> 00:23:37,827
A hits in way 0 in PLRU. Way 0 is on the left of B1 and on the left of B0. So both bits become 1, and the state is 1 1 0.

201
00:23:38,277 --> 00:23:44,976
A also hits in true LRU. A becomes the newest block. The oldest block is now B.

202
00:23:45,426 --> 00:23:53,426
E misses. The PLRU walk on the state 1 1 0 ends at way 2. So C, in way 2, is evicted.

203
00:23:53,876 --> 00:23:57,908
True LRU evicts its oldest block, B, in way 1.

204
00:23:58,358 --> 00:24:03,094
B comes back. PLRU still holds B, in way 1, so it is a hit.

205
00:24:03,544 --> 00:24:10,264
True LRU evicted B, so it is a miss. True LRU now evicts C, its oldest block.

206
00:24:10,714 --> 00:24:18,202
F misses in PLRU. The walk on the state 1 0 1 ends at way 3. So D is evicted.

207
00:24:18,652 --> 00:24:23,857
F also misses in true LRU. It evicts D, its oldest block.

208
00:24:24,307 --> 00:24:28,105
Count the misses. PLRU has 2 misses: E and F.

209
00:24:28,555 --> 00:24:31,989
True LRU has 3 misses: E, B and F.

210
00:24:32,439 --> 00:24:41,911
So PLRU is not always worse. It is an approximation: it can choose a different victim, and the result can be better or worse.

211
00:24:42,361 --> 00:24:53,817
Here is the recap. One. True LRU with age counts needs N times log 2 of N bits per set. Every access compares all ages. It is expensive.

212
00:24:54,267 --> 00:25:03,846
Two. PLRU uses a binary tree of N minus 1 bits per set. For 4 ways, the bits are B0, B1 and B2.

213
00:25:04,296 --> 00:25:11,997
Three. A bit of 0 means that the left side is LRU. A bit of 1 means that the right side is LRU.

214
00:25:12,447 --> 00:25:19,850
Four. On a miss, walk from the root. Go left on 0 and right on 1. The way at the end is the victim.

215
00:25:20,300 --> 00:25:28,535
Five. On a hit or a fill, set each bit on the path to point away from the used way. The other bits keep their value.

216
00:25:28,985 --> 00:25:38,563
Six. PLRU is an approximation. It can evict a block that is not the true least recently used block, as on slide 11.

217
00:25:39,013 --> 00:25:49,275
Seven. Check the slide text against the drawings. Slides 7 and 8 have typos, but their results are correct. That is the end of video 16.
